# ALiSiO Multi-Tenant PMS - Centralized Environment Configuration

# ─── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DB_DRIVER is what chooses the engine. DATABASE_URL only supplies the
# connection string, and on its own does nothing at all.
#
# This file used to list DATABASE_URL and not DB_DRIVER, which is the most
# expensive omission it could make: whoever copied the example got a filled-in
# Postgres connection string and every reason to believe the application was
# running on Postgres, while getSql() (src/core/db/async.ts) looks at DB_DRIVER
# alone and silently kept them on SQLite. Nothing fails — the data simply goes
# somewhere other than where the person thinks it goes.
#
# Two variables on purpose: DATABASE_URL is inherited from a scaffold and still
# sits in at least one developer's .env.local pointing at a Postgres that is not
# running. Keying the engine on it would let a forgotten line switch a live
# install. Set DB_DRIVER=postgres deliberately, or leave it empty for SQLite.
DB_DRIVER=
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/alisio_pms?sslmode=disable"

# ─── System URLs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Legacy spelling of APP_URL below, still read by core/app-url.ts so existing
# deployments keep working. New installs should set APP_URL instead.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="https://pms.example.com"

# ─── AI Integrations (OpenAI) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Guest content translation and passport / ID OCR. Both fail quietly without it
# rather than loudly — see deploy/env.prod.example for what that looks like.
OPENAI_API_KEY="sk-proj-..."

# ─── Payment gateways: NOT here ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is no STRIPE_SECRET_KEY (or GoPay, or any other gateway key) in this
# file, and there must not be one. A hotel enters its own gateway keys on
# /app/settings/payments; they are stored encrypted in `channel_credentials`
# through seal(), owner-only, scoped to that organization — the same mechanism
# as every other integration secret (src/core/payments.ts).
#
# A server-wide environment variable would be one gateway account shared by
# every tenant on the instance: one hotel's guests paying into another hotel's
# merchant account.
#
# Separately, a saved key does not yet mean cards can be charged. Every
# provider in src/core/payments.ts carries live: false, because the code that
# talks to the gateway does not exist. The empty STRIPE_SECRET_KEY that used to
# stand here suggested a key was the only thing missing.

# ─── Core ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public origin of this deployment. Used for guest links and invoice links.
# Without it those links are relative — never a foreign domain.
APP_URL=

# Encryption key for integration credentials stored in the database (IMAP
# passwords, channel API keys, payment gateway keys). 64 hex chars:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
APP_SECRET_KEY=

# ─── Channex (channel manager) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The one integration key that DOES belong in this file, and the reason is
# worth stating because the payment-gateway note above says the opposite.
#
# A gateway key belongs to the hotel: money lands in the hotel's merchant
# account, so a server-wide value would pay one tenant into another's account.
# The Channex key belongs to US. Channex's own PMS guide has the PMS hold one
# account and add every hotel inside it as a `property`, and the White Label
# price is built that way — a fixed monthly fee plus per property. So there is
# nothing tenant-scoped to protect: one key, many properties, and the tenant
# boundary is the `property_id` on every call.
#
# If a hotel ever brings its OWN Channex account, that key does NOT come here.
# It goes into `channel_credentials` through seal(), like every other
# per-tenant secret, and the code picks the tenant's key over this one.
#
# The key is shown once, on creation, in the Channex profile area.
#
# 02.09.2026: the APPLICATION no longer reads this variable at all — the hotel's
# key is entered in the connection wizard and stored in `channel_credentials`
# as `channel_manager`. Only the operator's live scripts
# (`scripts/channex-*-live.mjs`) read CHANNEX_API_KEY / CHANNEX_ENV.
CHANNEX_API_KEY=
# staging | production. Empty means staging — the safe default: a wrong guess
# writes to a sandbox instead of a live hotel's Booking.com listing.
#   staging     → https://staging.channex.io/api/v1
#   production  → https://app.channex.io/api/v1
CHANNEX_ENV=

# ─── Scheduled jobs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without these the /api/cron/* routes refuse with 503 rather than running for
# anyone who asks. That is deliberate: they used to fall back to a default
# printed in the source, and docker-compose never passed them in, so the
# fallback was the live configuration.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
CRON_SECRET=
# Its own value, so handing a channel manager a URL does not hand it the crontab.
ICAL_CRON_SECRET=
